WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.754
Day 9 left you with a clean branch on your laptop.

2
00:00:05.754 --> 00:00:08.687
You could merge it into main yourself, and push.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.130
Then nobody else reads the change before it lands.

4
00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.908
And no test runs on it, unless you ran one.

5
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:17.500
A pull request fixes both.

6
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.848
It is a page on GitHub that asks to merge your branch into main.

7
00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.011
Teammates review the diff there.

8
00:00:24.011 --> 00:00:28.629
And checks run on it: automatic tests that show a green tick or a red cross.

9
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:31.422
But what exactly do the checks test?

10
00:00:31.422 --> 00:00:33.148
Your branch, as it sits?

11
00:00:33.148 --> 00:00:33.867
Not quite.

12
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.163
Two teammates start from the same main, on the same morning.

13
00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:42.363
Teammate A renames a function, count_visit, to record_visit.

14
00:00:42.363 --> 00:00:44.161
Its one caller changes too.

15
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:48.898
Teammate B adds a new endpoint, /hello/<name>, and a test for it.

16
00:00:48.898 --> 00:00:52.593
It calls count_visit, which was correct that morning.

17
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.427
A opens pull request 12.

18
00:00:54.427 --> 00:00:55.987
Here is the first fact.

19
00:00:55.987 --> 00:01:01.140
GitHub builds a temporary merge commit, of the branch into the current main.

20
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:03.390
CI tests that combination.

21
00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.658
Your branch, plus main as it is right now.

22
00:01:06.658 --> 00:01:07.125
Green.

23
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.334
B opens pull request 13.

24
00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:12.836
Same thing: B's branch plus the same main.

25
00:01:12.836 --> 00:01:13.753
Also green.

26
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:15.997
A's pull request merges first.

27
00:01:15.997 --> 00:01:20.465
Main moves on, to a new commit where count_visit no longer exists.

28
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:22.018
Now the second fact.

29
00:01:22.018 --> 00:01:26.408
CI starts only when something happens to the pull request itself.

30
00:01:26.408 --> 00:01:29.583
It is opened, gets new commits, or is reopened.

31
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.903
A new commit on main is not an event on B's pull request.

32
00:01:33.903 --> 00:01:35.199
So nothing reruns.

33
00:01:35.199 --> 00:01:37.863
Its green tick still says main at m1.

34
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:39.176
So B merges too.

35
00:01:39.176 --> 00:01:44.309
Git reports no conflict, because the two branches changed different lines.

36
00:01:44.533 --> 00:01:46.413
Then the tests run on main.

37
00:01:46.413 --> 00:01:50.310
The new endpoint calls a function that no longer exists.

38
00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.145
Main is red.

39
00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:53.202
This is a semantic conflict.

40
00:01:53.202 --> 00:01:56.415
The text merged cleanly, and the meaning did not.

41
00:01:56.633 --> 00:01:59.226
Two green pull requests, one broken main.

42
00:01:59.226 --> 00:02:00.871
What would have caught it?

43
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.926
Rewind to before B merged.

44
00:02:03.926 --> 00:02:09.964
A ruleset option, Require branches to be up to date before merging, changes the story.

45
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:12.257
B's branch is behind main now.

46
00:02:12.257 --> 00:02:15.812
So pull request 13 must take in the new main first.

47
00:02:15.812 --> 00:02:18.251
The Update branch button does that.

48
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:24.831
That is a new commit on the pull request, so CI runs again, on B's branch plus the new main.

49
00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:28.808
The test fails on the pull request, instead of on main.

50
00:02:28.808 --> 00:02:29.975
Main stays green.

51
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:39.056
So remember: a pull request's checks test your branch merged into main, as main was the last time the pull request changed.
